(i)$, i.e. $i\\\\in S_b$.  Moreover the rows of $R$ form a contiguous interval $I\\\\subseteq S_b$.  Conversely, if a cell $(i,b)$ belongs to $L$, the rectangle that contains it cannot have a rightmost column larger than $b$ (otherwise it would also contain a cell $(i,c)$ with $c>b$ and $c<\\\\pi(i)$, contradicting the definition of $b$).  Hence the rectangles whose rightmost column equals $b$ partition $S_b$ into disjoint row‑intervals.\\n\\nConsequently the number of rectangles covering $L$ equals\\n\\\\[\\\\sum_{j=1}^{n-1}\\\\kappa_j,\\\\qquad\\\\kappa_j:=\\\\text{number of connected components (row‑intervals) of }S_j.\\\\]\\nSince each $S_j$ is non‑empty, $\\\\kappa_j\\\\ge1$ for all $j$, and we obtain the lower bound\\n\\\\[\\\\#\\\\text{tiles covering }L\\\\ge\\\\sum_{j=1}^{n-1}1=n-1.\\\\]\\n\\n---\\n### 4.  A lower bound for the right part $R$\\nThe argument is symmetric.  For $c\\\\in\\\\{2,\\\\dots,n\\\\}$ define\\n\\\\[T_c=\\\\{i\\\\mid \\\\pi(i)<c\\\\}.\\n\\\\]\\nThe cells of $R$ in column $c$ are $\\\\{(i,c)\\\\mid i\\\\in T_c\\\\}$.  Because $\\\\pi$ is a permutation there is a row $i_*$ with $\\\\pi(i_*)=1$, and $i_*\\\\in T_c$ for every $c\\\\ge2$, so each $T_c$ is non‑empty.\\n\\nRepeating the previous reasoning with the *leftmost* column of a rectangle shows that the rectangles covering $R$ are in one‑to‑one correspondence with the connected components of the sets $T_c$, and therefore\\n\\\\[\\\\#\\\\text{tiles covering }R\\\\ge\\\\sum_{c=2}^{n}1=n-1.\\n\\\\]\\n\\n---\\n### 5.  Global lower bound\\nAdding the two estimates yields\\n\\\\[\\\\#\\\\text{tiles}\\\\ge (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\\\\]\\nThus **any** placement satisfying the problem’s condition needs at least $2n-2$ tiles.\\n\\n---\\n### 6.  A construction attaining $2n-2$ tiles (upper bound)\\nChoose the *cyclic shift* permutation\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\pi(i)=\\\\begin{cases}i+1,&1\\\\le i\\\\le n-1,\\\\\\\\[2pt]1,&i=n.\\\\end{cases}\\n\\\\]\\nThe uncovered squares are\\n\\\\[(1,2),(2,3),\\\\dots,(n-1,n),(n