ctangles, and the lower bound shows no fewer are possible.  Therefore the minimum number of tiles is $2n-2=2\\cdot2025-2=4048$.\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\n**1. Reduction to the diagonal case**\nLet $n=2025$.  Let $p:\\{1,\\dots,n\\}\\to\\{1,\\dots,n\\}$ be the permutation defined by the condition that the unique uncovered square in row $i$ lies in column $p(i)$.  Apply the column permutation $\\sigma$ defined by $\\sigma(p(i))=i$ for all $i$.  This permutation merely reorders the columns of the grid, which does not affect the number of tiles needed.  After the reordering the uncovered squares are exactly the diagonal cells $(i,i)$, $i=1,\\dots,n$.\n\nThus we must partition the set\n$$\\mathcal{O}=\\{(i,j):1\\le i,j\\le n,\\;i\\neq j\\}$$\ninto axis\u2011aligned rectangles that avoid the diagonal.  A rectangle is determined by a pair of subsets $R,C\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots,n\\}$ with $R\\cap C=\\varnothing$; it consists of all cells $(i,j)$ with $i\\in R$, $j\\in C$.\n\n**2. Upper bound \u2013 explicit tiling with $2n-2$ tiles**\nDefine the following $2n-2$ rectangles.\n- For each $i\\in\\{1,\\dots,n-1\\}$ let\n  $$H_i=\\{(i,j):j\\in\\{i+1,i+2,\\dots,n\\}\\}.$$\n  $H_i$ is a horizontal rectangle covering row $i$ and all columns to the right of the diagonal.\n- For each $i\\in\\{2,\\dots,n\\}$ let\n  $$V_i=\\{(j,i):j\\in\\{1,2,\\dots,i-1\\}\\}.$$\n  $V_i$ is a vertical rectangle covering column $i$ and all rows above the diagonal.\nThese rectangles are pairwise disjoint because $H_i$ uses only columns $>i$ while $V_j$ uses only rows $<j$, and the two families meet only on the diagonal, which is excluded.  Every off\u2011diagonal cell $(i,j)$ with $i<j$ belongs to $H_i$, and every cell with $i>j$ belongs to $V_j$.  Hence the $2n-2$ rectangles cover all cells of $\\mathcal{O}$ exactly once.  Consequently\n$$f(n)\\le 2n-2.$$\n\n**3. Lower bound \u2013 induction**\nWe prove that any partition of $\\mathcal{O}$ into rectangles must contain at least $2n-2$ rectangles.\n\n*Base case $n=2$.*  The set $\\mathcal{O}=\\{(1,2),(2,1)\\}$.  The two cells cannot lie in a common rectangle, because a rectangle containing both would have $R=\\{1,2\\}$ and $C=\\{1,2\\}$, which would also contain the diagonal cell $(1,1)$ (or $(2,2)$), contradicting the requirement.  Hence at least two rectangles are needed, and $2n-2=2$.\n\n*Inductive step.*  Assume the claim holds for all sizes $<n$ and consider an optimal partition $\\mathcal{P}$ of $\\mathcal{O}$ for size $n$.\n\nRow $1$ contains the cells $(1,j)$ for $j=2,\\dots,n$.  Since a rectangle cannot contain both row $1$ and column $1$, every rectangle that covers a cell of row $1$ must have row $1\\in R$ and column $1\\notin C$.  Let $\\mathcal{P}_R$ be the subfamily of $\\mathcal{P}$ consisting of those rectangles; their column sets are pairwise disjoint and together form a partition of $\\{2,\\dots,n\\}$.  In particular $\\mathcal{P}_R$ is non\u2011empty.\n\nSimilarly, column $1$ contains the cells $(i,1)$ for $i=2,\\dots,n$.  Any rectangle covering one of these cells must have column $1\\in C$ and row $1\\notin R$.  Let $\\mathcal{P}_C$ be the subfamily of such rectangles; their row sets are pairwise disjoint and partition $\\{2,\\dots,n\\}$.  Thus $\\mathcal{P}_C$ is also non\u2011empty.\n\nAll rectangles in $\\mathcal{P}_R\\cup\\mathcal{P}_C$ involve either row $1$ or column $1$ (but never both).  Consequently none of them contains any cell whose both coordinates lie in $\\{2,\\dots,n\\}$.  Therefore the remaining rectangles $\\mathcal{P}'=\\mathcal{P}\\setminus(\\mathcal{P}_R\\cup\\mathcal{P}_C)$ form a partition of the off\u2011diagonal cells of the $(n-1)\\times(n-1)$ subgrid consisting of rows $2,\\dots,n$ and columns $2,\\dots,n$.\n\nBy the induction hypothesis, $|\\mathcal{P}'|\\ge 2(n-1)-2 = 2n-4$.  Adding the at least one rectangle from $\\mathcal{P}_R$ and the at least one rectangle from $\\mathcal{P}_C$ we obtain\n$$|\\mathcal{P}|\\;=\\;|\\mathcal{P}'|+|\\mathcal{P}_R|+|\\mathcal{P}_C