ows 1 to i-1 (contiguous interval). So they are indeed rectangles. So construction is valid.\n\nCheck that each row and column has exactly one uncovered square. The uncovered squares are diagonal cells (i,i). In each row i, the uncovered cell is (i,i). In each column i, same. So condition satisfied.\n\nNow lower bound: Induction argument appears plausible. Need to check any logical errors. The argument: For any partition of off-diagonal cells into rectangles, they consider row 1 and column 1. They argue that any rectangle covering a cell in row 1 cannot include column 1, so must have row 1 in R and column 1 not in C. Then the column sets of those rectangles are pairwise disjoint and partition {2,...,n}. Similarly for column 1. Then they remove those rectangles and consider remaining off-diagonal cells of subgrid (rows 2..n, columns 2..n). By induction, need at least 2(n-1)-2 = 2n-4 rectangles. Then add at least one from each of the two families, total >=2n-2. This seems correct. Need to check that there is at least one rectangle in each family. Since row 1 has n-1 cells, they must be covered by at least one rectangle. So yes. Similarly column 1.\n\nPotential subtlety: Could a rectangle covering a cell in row 1 also cover a cell in column 1? No, because that would include diagonal cell (1,1). So they can't. So families are disjoint.\n\nThus lower bound seems valid.\n\nThus answer appears correct.\n\nNow Level 1: Since it's optimization, we must check for arithmetic errors or constraint violations. No errors. So proceed to Level 1.5.\n\nLevel 1.5: Check optimality. The solution claims answer = 2n-2. We need to test small cases to see if any better solution exists. For n=2, answer = 2. Indeed need 2 tiles (two uncovered cells). Could we do with 1 tile? No, because need each row and column have exactly one uncovered cell, so for n=2 we need uncovered cells at (1,1) and (2,2). The off-diagonal cells are (1,2) and (2,1). Could we cover both with a single rectangle? That r