ngles:\\n\\n* Left rectangle $L_k$: rows $k+1,\\\\dots,n$, columns $1,\\\\dots,k$.  Its dimensions are $(n-k)\\\\times k$.\\n* Right rectangle $R_k$: rows $1,\\\\dots,k$, columns $k+1,\\\\dots,n$.  Its dimensions are $k\\\\times (n-k)$.\\n\\nBecause $p(i)=i$, for any row $i\\\\ge k+1$ we have $p(i)=i>k$, so all columns $1,\\\\dots,k$ lie strictly left of the uncovered square $(i,i)$.  Hence $L_k$ does not intersect any uncovered square.  Similarly, for any row $i\\\\le k$ we have $p(i)=i\\\\le k$, so all columns $k+1,\\\\dots,n$ lie strictly right of $(i,i)$, and $R_k$ avoids all uncovered squares.\\n\\nThe families $\\\\{L_k\\\\}$ and $\\\\{R_k\\\\}$ are pairwise disjoint: any $L_k$ uses only columns $\\\\le k$, while any $R_{k'}$ uses only columns $>k'$.  Moreover, each $L_k$ occupies rows $k+1,\\\\dots,n$, which are disjoint from the rows of $L_{k'}$ for $k'\\\\neq k$, and similarly for the $R_k$.  Consequently the $2(n-1)$ rectangles tile every square of the board except the $n$ diagonal squares, and no two rectangles overlap.\\n\\nThus we have exhibited a tiling with exactly $2n-2$ tiles.  Combined with the lower bound, this shows that the minimum possible number of tiles is\\n\\\\[\\n2n-2 = 2\\\\cdot2025-2 = 4048.\\n\\\\]\\n\\n---\\n**Conclusion**\\n\\nThe minimum number of rectangular tiles required is $4048$.\\n\"\n}"
