um over j of kappa_j, where kappa_j is number of row-intervals into which S_j is split. Since each S_j is non-empty, kappa_j >= 1. So total tiles covering L >= sum_{j=1}^{n-1} 1 = n-1.\n\nSimilarly for R, define T_c = {i | pi(i) < c} for each column c from 2 to n. Tiles covering R have leftmost column c, and rows form intervals partitioning T_c. So #tiles covering R >= sum_{c=2}^{n} 1 = n-1.\n\nThus total tiles >= 2n-2.\n\nPotential issues: Are we sure each S_j is non-empty? For any j from 1 to n-1, there exists at least one i such that pi(i) > j. Since pi is a permutation of {1,...,n}, there is exactly one i for each value of pi(i). For j = n-1, there must be at least one i with pi(i) = n (since pi is a permutation). So S_{n-1} contains that i. For any j < n-1, there is at least one i with pi(i) = n (or any value > j). So S_j is non-empty. So that part holds.\n\nNow the argument that each tile in L has a rightmost column b, and that the rows of the tile form a contiguous interval, and that the set of rows in S_b are partitioned by the tiles with rightmost column b. This seems correct: For each column j, the tiles in L that end at column j cover exactly those rows i for which pi(i) > j, i.e., S_j. Since each tile is a rectangle, its rows are contiguous. So the number of tiles with rightmost column j is at least the number of intervals needed to cover S_j. Since S_j is a subset of rows, which is a set of indices, the minimal number of intervals needed to cover S_j is the number of connected components of S_j when considered as a subset of {1,...,n} with adjacency defined by consecutive rows. The number of intervals is at least 1 if S_j is non-empty. So the bound holds.\n\nBut could there be a tile in L that has rightmost column b but also includes rows i where pi(i) <= b? No, because if pi(i) <= b, then column b is >= pi(i). But tile includes column b, which is at least pi(i). If pi(i) = b, then tile includes the uncovered square (i,b) which is not allowed. If pi(i) 